<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0657">Example <span data-tag="0657">9.3.2.11</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{Set}$ be the category of sets, and let $\operatorname{Set}_{\mathrm{fin}}$ be the full subcategory spanned by the finite sets. Then the inclusion functor $\operatorname{Set}_{\mathrm{fin}} \hookrightarrow \operatorname{Set}$ exhibits (the nerve of) $\operatorname{Set}$ as an $\operatorname{Ind}$-completion of the $\operatorname{Set}_{\mathrm{fin}}$. This follows from Corollary <a data-tag="0656" href="/tag/0656">9.3.2.10</a>, since every finite set is a compact object of $\operatorname{Set}$ (Example <a data-tag="063D" href="/tag/063D">9.2.0.4</a>) and every set can be realized as a filtered colimit of finite sets. </p>
</article>
